    11	## Ch14 — Vastin (cutaway, ~5k; his POV only; no age; "forty years"; "the Archmarshal")
    12	
    13	1. Morning, day 41. The high room in the Compact's house: the too-steep stair, the porter and his paper bag in the paved court, the long cabinet of shallow drawers, the seal-press he screwed on crooked his first morning nineteen years ago, three trays (in, held, done). The left hand stiff — it used to object only to mornings; now it objects at both ends of the day. In the in-tray, between a frame-maintenance return from a river station and a leave request from a district clerk: a bundle with a printed distribution slip — *for the evaluator's file, per standing distribution*. Information, not a question. Every paper about this boy for three years came to him asking; this one tells. Inside: the instrument, the house's answer (its index only), the seat's notice, and the first sitting's minute (five evaluation chairs, "to be called"). The sitting has already happened. He learns of the proceedings after they have begun.
    14	2. He reads the dull items first (his rule: interesting things last, so his judgment on the dull ones stays honest). Then the instrument at his own pace. Paragraph seven: his own three sentences, quoted whole and "adopted". He feels it as a signature he never wrote. He knows the corridor's architecture because he has walked two people down it: chapter fourteen triggered twice by findings of his, both correct. A clerk who sold sponsorship seals; an assessor paid to alter a station entry. The second man's daughter wrote to him months after, asking if her father's name would ever come off the list. He answered: no. He looks at the court for exactly as long as he allows, and stops.
    15	3. He takes the boy's file from his own drawer, not from memory. His portion: nine leaves. The clerk's ruled column: eleven questions, numbered; the minutes down the margin; the boy's one question back (what is being evaluated, him or the provision). The twelfth: what the boy's private records say. He left it because asking would have been collecting, not evaluating, and he made a promise in a rainy corridor long ago. Still right. He notices that his rightness leaves the proceedings nothing to use except his three sentences, which they have already used.
    16	4. He drafts a petition for standing: the senior evaluator who personally assessed the subject asks leave to submit a finding. Two provisions, both old; the older one from before the standardizing, which uses *shall receive*. He cites both; a petition on one leg deserves to fall. He sets out his history from memory: four supplementary findings in forty years, four granted, each received and weighed — one decisive against the petitioning district's own case, and the district wrote to thank him. He predicts: standing within days, a finding entered at the next sitting.
    17	5. The clerk at the dispatch ledger hesitates at the routing column; Vastin supplies the route ("Registry seat, Ostrand. Proceedings. Institutional."). The clerk has never sent paper from this desk *toward* a proceeding. Vastin notes the hesitation and what it says.
    18	6. Day 43: a junior evaluator from the northern district brings a hard file — a station's Kindling entry against a young practitioner whose shown work outruns it. They go through it for an hour; inside tolerance; growth faster than the station guessed; *continue observation*. The junior leaves comforted. Vastin keeps the copy on the blotter after, reads one line too many times, puts it in "held", takes it out, puts it back. No word for it; it is not yet a thing. [plant; unnamed]
    19	7. Day 45, the fourth morning: the clerk's tread on the stair is wrong — single steps from the bottom, no pause at the door. Seat courier, not the post. Impressed seal, not inked (a grade of reply). [P] *The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.* He reads it standing, sits, reads it twice more; on the third he hears it as a sentence he could have written — he has written hundreds of its cousins, closing doors cleanly so the person outside has nothing to hold. Good craft; among his best. Now aimed at him. No provision cited. It cost its sender nothing. "Fully constituted" — a seat he was not asked to sit on was full before he asked. He puts the sheet in *held* — no; in the boy's file.
    20	8. He writes the finding anyway that evening, in his own hand, the addressee line left empty. The building's lamp rule (the floor's lamps out at the sixth bell). The clerk's one sentence in nineteen years that is not about the work — his own question (I'll give him: whether the Archmarshal would like the window shut, the pigeons are loud — no: a question that touches the man: "Will you be going home tonight, sir?"). Candle fetched and entered on the consumables under Vastin's name. The finding: the standard protocol cannot measure this subject; its silence is the instrument's limit, not the subject's fault; a re-evaluation whose result is known in advance is a procedure dressed as an evaluation; the honest instrument available is the demonstration record. The hardest page: the instruments are not at fault — the same sentence he has defended all his life for frames, now for a boy. Last paragraph: the schedule's purpose unknown to him; an evaluation that starts from its conclusion evaluates the evaluator. Nine pages. Into his own drawer in the cabinet of shallow drawers, under a heading he never needed before: [P] *Pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*
    21	
    22	## Ch15 — Sideways (day 44)
    23	
    24	1. Afternoon: the assistant back from an Ostrand archive errand with orders to buy one of every printed thing about the case and account for it. Four sheets in her hand, a receipt pinned. Six titles in Ostrand alone; the old assay transcript in a fourth printing; a new pamphlet claiming the whole instrument and getting the back half wrong; the same woodcut of a boy who isn't him; two weekly broadside series at a copper. She asked the seller what sold before; he said the tournament; before that, nothing — the series began for him, by a printer on the wharf street who staked his press on it and has bought a second. Karis: a family in Ostrand eats because you can't be classified; put it in your book, not mine. Cael prices it: a working man's year, every week, spent on the question of what he is. Log: they're buying the question, not an answer; that market outlives the ruling.
    25	2. Dusk: the counsel's hired chaise on the switchbacks, unexpected. She heard at noon in Ostrand, from seat clerks talking at the inn by the wharfs: the Archmarshal petitioned for standing to submit a finding; refused; two sentences; no provision cited. She tells Withrow in her office; Withrow has Cael sent for to hear it. Withrow asks for the sentence twice. Then: she has written that sentence; every registry officer of her grade has; she taught it to new clerks as the courteous way to refuse — saying no without pretending to weigh. She now has to think about what she taught. To Cael: tell the table; tell them I said *choosing* again.
    26	3. Cael's arithmetic on the stair: the refusal was written in Ostrand this morning and the man it refuses is still waiting for it on a road somewhere; the house has heard first. [own beat]
    27	4. The annex: Karis already has the two provisions open (only two exist; one older than the standardizing). She reads them aloud before anyone talks. The older says *shall receive*. Her count of their use: a handful of times in the record, granted every time. "He cited the right law. They answered without citing any." Not a ruling — a schedule declining to contain one.
    28	5. The counsel, gloves on, at the door: she will not have them think the Archmarshal is theirs; he is an officer of the Compact refused by an office of the Compact. What she gives them: in twenty-six years she has been refused often and refused without a citation only a few times, and each time the office had already decided and would not write down why. Write the citation down when you get one. [P] "Its absence is a finding."
    29	6. Supper, the residence table: nobody says much. Seln, at the bench's middle, cup turned: [P] "They benched their best instrument because he measures true." Brom looks at him a long moment. On the stair after, unasked (rare): Brom says Seln meant the Archmarshal — and himself too: fifteen years, the best they had, set on a shelf by his own true reports. Cael writes it down; so does Karis (she was on the stair above).
    30	7. Log: Vastin warned the channels would change; they changed on him too. The proceedings didn't dispute his finding — they declined to read it. The only two credentialed opinions ever made about me, both set aside. They are learning to send people I can't read and to put away the ones I can.
    31	
    32	## Ch16 — The Asking (days 43–45; builds over the weeks since service)
    33	
    34	1. Correspondents up the bluff road twice since the sitting; the lodge refuses them on Withrow's standing line, which Bracken posted at the gate in registry type (own words: the house's answer is its filing, and the filing is public). Second time (day 45), one waits at the switchback till dark; Rooke, coming down on his own errand, stops and gives him one sentence [own: He trains before light and eats after dark, and neither happens on this road]. The man goes. That's the house's whole press policy.
    35	2. The post: two sacks a day at Bracken's counter. Bracken builds a procedure on day two, not a complaint on day ten: a second table in the records hall's outer room, four chalked cards, the day's count in his counter daybook each night. Heaviest day 211 items. Fourteen from chartered academies — he answers those himself, identically, in two lines (own text). Shows Cael one. Institutions write about the provision; the continent writes about the boy; your friend sorts that.
    36	3. Lira sorts the boy's pile at the residence table each evening: academies to Bracken's tray, sponsorships to Withrow's, faculties to Karis, the rest in three piles named once: [P] *Kind. Unkind. Unhinged.* The kind pile biggest and oddest (a barge widow; a washed-out Copper with four pages on corridors; a child in a schoolroom hand asking if it hurts not to have a Path written down). Lira reads the child's aloud once. Brom fills cups. Karis: answer none, keep all — the kind pile will be the year's only record not written by an office.
    37	4. Ephram at the table with the day's sack: you were a story at Norhold, a story ends; now you're a case, cases get taught. [own Ephram joke: Try to be a dull precedent.]
    38	5. The cost lands on the others first; Cael misses it for nine days. (a) Lira: the program clerk has logged the east-hall gallery on Rooke's order — twenty-odd names in a fortnight, four from houses with no business here, one (a Rhagen man) asking the clerk in a friendly way how often the unclassified enrollee came to watch. Rooke reads the log and tells the clerk to keep logging. (b) Brom: another Velmere letter, sooner than the last; read at the table, folded, pocketed, no word. (c) Karis: her Greyvane request, routed through the district fee office at Ostrand for its seal, comes back with a printed query asking her to confirm her research standing. Proper. Never asked in years of requests. "Once is a careful clerk. I'll say if it's twice." (d) The assistant, stopped on the covered walk by a second-year she barely knows, answered at length and correctly (lawful, adjudicated on the public record, the grant and two renewals on paper) and came to Karis afraid she'd been [P] "boring". Karis: you were accurate at speed; boring is the job; be boring a year.
    39	6. That night: Cael opens Hesk's book at the ruled column with four names at its head (from M2) and fills it for the first time — three of the four have a line; his own has none. Log: I wrote that it should not cost them; it costs them first and me nothing yet. The order is the thing I got wrong.
    40	
    41	## Ch17 — Under the Gallery (days 46–47)
    42	
    43	1. Day 46 Fourth-day: sitting week, so the provision's bout is moved to the Fourth-day. Wing three has a narrow old gallery across its west end over the door (when the hall was the house's examination room; lately stacked with benches). The porters cleared it on the wing clerk's order because guests had signed the lodge book. Four visitors in it with boards: two from Rhagen (the house that writes everything down and sells it), one from the lake house, one from a northern house. Rooke at the east rail beside Ephram, arms folded, not there by schedule.
    44	2. Cael's rotation line written last night (own: the first exchange to the Current, two bursts only, the spark once; buys a figure near the middle). The problem: strangers comparing him against a printed record. Look exactly like the house's record — not more, not less, not the same as last week either.
    45	3. The bout (~1,800): a Current Path third-year, Iron Rank Four, long gliding steps that carry her farther than her legs should; two glides, the second bending, a strike off the bend. E1: he reads her weight, sees the bend coming, could burst inside it — gives her E1 as the line says; takes the strike on the forearm the honest way. E2: he uses the read to stand where the bend ends; one burst; touch. E3: she adapts — glides straight, no bend; he spends the spark on her lead wrist; no touch; glass runs. E4: she shortens to a single glide; he must not use a third burst? (line allows two); he takes ground on foot, lets her come to the wall, touch on her shoulder as she turns. Two to one. Throughout: the Rhagen pencils moving when he moves. He feels the band as a costume the strangers are measuring.
    46	4. Figure: Gault adds the cards — twenty-two and two-thirds. Ephram writes it, no question mark this time; then, quietly: "They wrote the same number I did. That's the first time anybody's agreed with me about you." Rooke goes straight out. By noon the east hall's shutters are drawn, the gallery stair chained, the program drills under lamps. Lira at dusk: he closed the floor; didn't tell me why; told the clerk to keep logging. Her light gone. Cael's column gets a second line under her name.
    47	5. Evening: Umber's transmission as a courtesy copy — the adjudication office's referred file sent entire to the proceedings (the record, the nine charts, the unscorable entry [canon: *Result: stoppage, Daeva, fourth exchange. Performance rating: unscorable under standard. Referred.*]) with a covering note (own text, three sentences: the file is sent entire; it is the whole of the office's finding; the office has nothing to add and asks nothing be added in its name). Karis maps it: once taken up the office had no choice; the third sentence is the only free act he had, and he used all of it; the clerk must read covering notes into the minute.
    48	6. Day 47 Fifth-day, the second sitting: an exhibits sitting. The clerk reads the house's 173 items into the minute — title, date, provenance, receipt. Cael times it: short ones nine seconds, certified ones twenty; Prynn's index; Gault's minutes (the grant and two renewals) together just over a minute; the inspection finding twenty-two seconds; Karis's forty pages, nine. Fifty-one minutes for all of it. Nobody disputes a line; Jent doesn't rise once except to enter Umber's file (one exhibit, cited by number). By minute forty a third of the gallery has stopped listening. Havel at the junior chair keeps times, does not look. Log: the truth goes in unopposed and puts the room to sleep; they've built a room where our best work is a delay.
    49	7. Counter, afternoon: Withrow signs the answer's release to three law faculties that asked; Bracken objects mildly (a respondent's filing is the respondent's). Withrow: a perfect filing nobody reads is a private loss; libraries outlast seats.
    50	8. Log: the honest men — Umber, Vastin, Ilsev — each wrote the truth, and each truth is now an exhibit for the petition. The machine runs on true reports the way a mill runs on a river: it doesn't ask where the water wanted to go.
