    52	## Ch18 — Fifteen Words (days 47–49)
    53	
    54	1. Day 47 supper. Bracken leaves his counter with nobody at it (first time Cael has seen it) and walks a sheet up to the residence faster than his own pace. Lays it before the counsel; stands; won't take Withrow's chair. The counsel reads it aloud: a statement entered at the seat in Daeva's own name and rank (own header: Daeva, of Auremont, Storm Path, Gold Rank Three, on her own account), then [P] *I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.* Silence. Lira sets her cup down. Brom reads it and turns it to face the room. Ephram: Auremont has a faculty for forty-page filings. Withrow: forty pages is an argument and arguments can be answered; this is a witness, from the top, and the code has no answer because nobody at her rank ever bothered. "Registrar. In your thirteen years?" [P-adj] "Never. Not at Gold." Four marks; a receipt.
    55	2. Karis, annex, later: a provision older than the standardizing lets anyone Silver or higher enter a statement on evaluation standards; used a few dozen times, by Silvers; never by a Gold — Golds are what the statements are about. *Classify the instruments* has no form. Will the seat care? No. The record will.
    56	3. Day 48 morning: Cael at the post room early. Seln is doing the sort (the post porter's shoulder). Seln hands him a pile to hold without a word; Cael watches how he sorts: three piles from the district courier's bag. Daeva's letter comes through Seln's hands — the hand from the *Overdue* form — Seln lays it on Cael's pile without looking at it. Cael reads it at the window: [P] the letter verbatim. Four readings. The one person the machine loves, telling him what the love is worth.
    57	4. Log: [P-adj] *Keep yourself unclassified* — from her, not a joke; a strategy; filed. He files it behind the front board of Hesk's book.
    58	5. Day 48 evening, Lira at the rail of the Crown yard: Zerin told her "Silver bracket. Next cycle." and walked off; she's been building toward it since. Daeva just did the same for him, from the top, on registry paper: here is where I'll be standing. Auremont builds them strange.
    59	6. Day 49 Seventh-day morning: Seln at the lodge counter for his quarter hour (the code lets the wing read the lodge book on the seventh day); Cael with him, uninvited, allowed. Tea; Seln talks to the lodge-man about the lodge-man's quarrel with a carter over a scraped gatepost. The book: arrivals, and the road column the lodge has kept every bell since a year of thieves (own detail). Cael's own week moved four times (down the bluff with Brom at an odd hour; the river path before light; the ferry landing with Karis's cases; the sitting). The road column: two at the foot, changed on the bell, every bell, unmoved. Cael gets it himself on the walk back: a watch that never shifts when its man shifts isn't on the man. [P] "He is holding a road." Seln: "Mm."
    60	
    61	## Ch19 — The Shape (day 49)
    62	
    63	1. Supper: Seln gives the table the finding, to the teapot, plain as an inventory. The two at the foot are holding the road and nothing else; the watching has moved since the second sitting onto what the boy is attached to. Four items: (i) a records request at this house's counter three weeks ago, its fourth item asking for every institutional relationship with the enrollee (Bracken answered completely, twice saying so); (ii) a query at the district seat on Lira's certification history "for completeness of the respondent's file"; (iii) a query at the provincial registry over Velmere on the household's standing and registered relationships; (iv) a query at Ternhall on Karis's transfer file. Each lawful; two of them the sort a keen junior files unasked. The pattern is the finding.
    64	2. Lira: her file has the Fenmark entry at the bottom, corrected over and never removed; a clerk read both halves in an afternoon. "I'd like it noted they've read it." Karis notes it. Brom's only question: [P] "Does the query show at the estate's end?" Seln: the provincial ledger is open to anyone entitled to the file; your grandmother is entitled. Brom nods once. Karis: the Ternhall one is twice. She writes four offices, dated, and rules one empty line under them.
    65	3. Lira on the stair: why the blank? Karis: four is chance, his family would make it a document; they haven't reached them yet; whoever is building this is good.
    66	4. The evening post, two hours later: the post porter puts a Denvash letter on top — he knows Hesk's upright address. Cael opens it walking on the covered walk (he opens only Hesk's walking; they've never been bad news). First paragraphs: Hesk's shop (own: a viol whose neck was set wrong by its maker and loved that way by its owner; Hesk spent days deciding to keep the fault; a price; the stove). Third paragraph: he stops on the walk, reads it again, goes to the residence at a pace nobody has seen from him in a year, and reads it aloud at the window: [P] *Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash…* Silence. He can't stop building the shop scene: front door, the bell, Hesk from the back with spectacles pushed up, answering exactly, offering nothing. Somebody wrote *grew up in it* on a form.
    67	5. Lira: "They went to Denvash." Seln lays one finger on the blank sheet in front of him where a fifth line would go. Karis writes the fifth office in her blank line.
    68	6. Seln names it, flat, his old trade: [P-adj] pre-restriction inventory. Before chapter fourteen's restriction executes, the registry maps everything the person's standing touches, so all of it shuts on one morning. Only run when the outcome is assumed. Karis: how long? "Six to ten weeks for an ordinary file. This one: [P] nine weeks. They are in the sixth." When finished: nothing — five lawful files in five offices; if the ruling goes his way, a clerk clears them in a few years and nobody did wrong; if not, assembly is an afternoon.
    69	7. Karis: the four were about us; the fifth is a man who has never been near an academy. He's in it because the ninth category is *other relationships* — the shape has to close round everything Cael wouldn't want touched. Same machine, second use. "Say out loud that we noticed." Brom: "Noticed."
    70	8. Lira declines the premise: Karis wins filings, the counsel wins sittings, Cael wins every point; their men are in Denvash. [P] "The question is what we do the day after." Nobody answers. Seln's silence is a different register.
    71	9. Withrow's office that night: Cael with the letter; she reads it standing; a pause mid-page (he counts it). Trade licences and title, on an instrument-maker. She sits, and sits him down (neither usual). The second restriction of her two — she ran its inventory herself, the district short of hands. Force Path, Bronze Rank Four, a haulage practitioner in a river district, wife, two children, a house mortgaged; his trouble a twenty-year-old station error nobody living could correct; the re-evaluation returned nothing. Nine categories, counted off (employment; guild standing; credentials; property; money and debts; enrollments, his own and his dependents'; travel endorsements; office or contract; other relationships). Seven weeks; proud of it. The morning: nothing happened *at* him — nine letters from four offices by the post; contract suspended, run given to another, the house's note frozen, the daughter's enrollment under review, the transit endorsement lapsed so he couldn't leave to argue. He came to her counter on the fourth day with all nine in a bag and asked which to answer first. One problem with nine faces and no counter to answer at — a condition, not an act. He went east past the last station in the end; records stop there. The daughter came every week for a year (matches M1). Hesk: *grew up in it*, entered under the ninth category, true; a licence held by a relation of a restricted person may be reviewed; reviewing is only slow. How slow? As slow as anyone likes. [P] "Not a hand on the door. A hand on the clock." She at the window: still choosing; it costs more from tonight; priced and paying (own words).
