## Ch14 — Vastin (cutaway, ~5k; his POV only; no age; "forty years"; "the Archmarshal")

1. Morning, day 41. The high room in the Compact's house: the too-steep stair, the porter and his paper bag in the paved court, the long cabinet of shallow drawers, the seal-press he screwed on crooked his first morning nineteen years ago, three trays (in, held, done). The left hand stiff — it used to object only to mornings; now it objects at both ends of the day. In the in-tray, between a frame-maintenance return from a river station and a leave request from a district clerk: a bundle with a printed distribution slip — *for the evaluator's file, per standing distribution*. Information, not a question. Every paper about this boy for three years came to him asking; this one tells. Inside: the instrument, the house's answer (its index only), the seat's notice, and the first sitting's minute (five evaluation chairs, "to be called"). The sitting has already happened. He learns of the proceedings after they have begun.
2. He reads the dull items first (his rule: interesting things last, so his judgment on the dull ones stays honest). Then the instrument at his own pace. Paragraph seven: his own three sentences, quoted whole and "adopted". He feels it as a signature he never wrote. He knows the corridor's architecture because he has walked two people down it: chapter fourteen triggered twice by findings of his, both correct. A clerk who sold sponsorship seals; an assessor paid to alter a station entry. The second man's daughter wrote to him months after, asking if her father's name would ever come off the list. He answered: no. He looks at the court for exactly as long as he allows, and stops.
3. He takes the boy's file from his own drawer, not from memory. His portion: nine leaves. The clerk's ruled column: eleven questions, numbered; the minutes down the margin; the boy's one question back (what is being evaluated, him or the provision). The twelfth: what the boy's private records say. He left it because asking would have been collecting, not evaluating, and he made a promise in a rainy corridor long ago. Still right. He notices that his rightness leaves the proceedings nothing to use except his three sentences, which they have already used.
4. He drafts a petition for standing: the senior evaluator who personally assessed the subject asks leave to submit a finding. Two provisions, both old; the older one from before the standardizing, which uses *shall receive*. He cites both; a petition on one leg deserves to fall. He sets out his history from memory: four supplementary findings in forty years, four granted, each received and weighed — one decisive against the petitioning district's own case, and the district wrote to thank him. He predicts: standing within days, a finding entered at the next sitting.
5. The clerk at the dispatch ledger hesitates at the routing column; Vastin supplies the route ("Registry seat, Ostrand. Proceedings. Institutional."). The clerk has never sent paper from this desk *toward* a proceeding. Vastin notes the hesitation and what it says.
6. Day 43: a junior evaluator from the northern district brings a hard file — a station's Kindling entry against a young practitioner whose shown work outruns it. They go through it for an hour; inside tolerance; growth faster than the station guessed; *continue observation*. The junior leaves comforted. Vastin keeps the copy on the blotter after, reads one line too many times, puts it in "held", takes it out, puts it back. No word for it; it is not yet a thing. [plant; unnamed]
7. Day 45, the fourth morning: the clerk's tread on the stair is wrong — single steps from the bottom, no pause at the door. Seat courier, not the post. Impressed seal, not inked (a grade of reply). [P] *The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.* He reads it standing, sits, reads it twice more; on the third he hears it as a sentence he could have written — he has written hundreds of its cousins, closing doors cleanly so the person outside has nothing to hold. Good craft; among his best. Now aimed at him. No provision cited. It cost its sender nothing. "Fully constituted" — a seat he was not asked to sit on was full before he asked. He puts the sheet in *held* — no; in the boy's file.
8. He writes the finding anyway that evening, in his own hand, the addressee line left empty. The building's lamp rule (the floor's lamps out at the sixth bell). The clerk's one sentence in nineteen years that is not about the work — his own question (I'll give him: whether the Archmarshal would like the window shut, the pigeons are loud — no: a question that touches the man: "Will you be going home tonight, sir?"). Candle fetched and entered on the consumables under Vastin's name. The finding: the standard protocol cannot measure this subject; its silence is the instrument's limit, not the subject's fault; a re-evaluation whose result is known in advance is a procedure dressed as an evaluation; the honest instrument available is the demonstration record. The hardest page: the instruments are not at fault — the same sentence he has defended all his life for frames, now for a boy. Last paragraph: the schedule's purpose unknown to him; an evaluation that starts from its conclusion evaluates the evaluator. Nine pages. Into his own drawer in the cabinet of shallow drawers, under a heading he never needed before: [P] *Pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*

## Ch15 — Sideways (day 44)

1. Afternoon: the assistant back from an Ostrand archive errand with orders to buy one of every printed thing about the case and account for it. Four sheets in her hand, a receipt pinned. Six titles in Ostrand alone; the old assay transcript in a fourth printing; a new pamphlet claiming the whole instrument and getting the back half wrong; the same woodcut of a boy who isn't him; two weekly broadside series at a copper. She asked the seller what sold before; he said the tournament; before that, nothing — the series began for him, by a printer on the wharf street who staked his press on it and has bought a second. Karis: a family in Ostrand eats because you can't be classified; put it in your book, not mine. Cael prices it: a working man's year, every week, spent on the question of what he is. Log: they're buying the question, not an answer; that market outlives the ruling.
2. Dusk: the counsel's hired chaise on the switchbacks, unexpected. She heard at noon in Ostrand, from seat clerks talking at the inn by the wharfs: the Archmarshal petitioned for standing to submit a finding; refused; two sentences; no provision cited. She tells Withrow in her office; Withrow has Cael sent for to hear it. Withrow asks for the sentence twice. Then: she has written that sentence; every registry officer of her grade has; she taught it to new clerks as the courteous way to refuse — saying no without pretending to weigh. She now has to think about what she taught. To Cael: tell the table; tell them I said *choosing* again.
3. Cael's arithmetic on the stair: the refusal was written in Ostrand this morning and the man it refuses is still waiting for it on a road somewhere; the house has heard first. [own beat]
4. The annex: Karis already has the two provisions open (only two exist; one older than the standardizing). She reads them aloud before anyone talks. The older says *shall receive*. Her count of their use: a handful of times in the record, granted every time. "He cited the right law. They answered without citing any." Not a ruling — a schedule declining to contain one.
5. The counsel, gloves on, at the door: she will not have them think the Archmarshal is theirs; he is an officer of the Compact refused by an office of the Compact. What she gives them: in twenty-six years she has been refused often and refused without a citation only a few times, and each time the office had already decided and would not write down why. Write the citation down when you get one. [P] "Its absence is a finding."
6. Supper, the residence table: nobody says much. Seln, at the bench's middle, cup turned: [P] "They benched their best instrument because he measures true." Brom looks at him a long moment. On the stair after, unasked (rare): Brom says Seln meant the Archmarshal — and himself too: fifteen years, the best they had, set on a shelf by his own true reports. Cael writes it down; so does Karis (she was on the stair above).
7. Log: Vastin warned the channels would change; they changed on him too. The proceedings didn't dispute his finding — they declined to read it. The only two credentialed opinions ever made about me, both set aside. They are learning to send people I can't read and to put away the ones I can.

## Ch16 — The Asking (days 43–45; builds over the weeks since service)

1. Correspondents up the bluff road twice since the sitting; the lodge refuses them on Withrow's standing line, which Bracken posted at the gate in registry type (own words: the house's answer is its filing, and the filing is public). Second time (day 45), one waits at the switchback till dark; Rooke, coming down on his own errand, stops and gives him one sentence [own: He trains before light and eats after dark, and neither happens on this road]. The man goes. That's the house's whole press policy.
2. The post: two sacks a day at Bracken's counter. Bracken builds a procedure on day two, not a complaint on day ten: a second table in the records hall's outer room, four chalked cards, the day's count in his counter daybook each night. Heaviest day 211 items. Fourteen from chartered academies — he answers those himself, identically, in two lines (own text). Shows Cael one. Institutions write about the provision; the continent writes about the boy; your friend sorts that.
3. Lira sorts the boy's pile at the residence table each evening: academies to Bracken's tray, sponsorships to Withrow's, faculties to Karis, the rest in three piles named once: [P] *Kind. Unkind. Unhinged.* The kind pile biggest and oddest (a barge widow; a washed-out Copper with four pages on corridors; a child in a schoolroom hand asking if it hurts not to have a Path written down). Lira reads the child's aloud once. Brom fills cups. Karis: answer none, keep all — the kind pile will be the year's only record not written by an office.
4. Ephram at the table with the day's sack: you were a story at Norhold, a story ends; now you're a case, cases get taught. [own Ephram joke: Try to be a dull precedent.]
5. The cost lands on the others first; Cael misses it for nine days. (a) Lira: the program clerk has logged the east-hall gallery on Rooke's order — twenty-odd names in a fortnight, four from houses with no business here, one (a Rhagen man) asking the clerk in a friendly way how often the unclassified enrollee came to watch. Rooke reads the log and tells the clerk to keep logging. (b) Brom: another Velmere letter, sooner than the last; read at the table, folded, pocketed, no word. (c) Karis: her Greyvane request, routed through the district fee office at Ostrand for its seal, comes back with a printed query asking her to confirm her research standing. Proper. Never asked in years of requests. "Once is a careful clerk. I'll say if it's twice." (d) The assistant, stopped on the covered walk by a second-year she barely knows, answered at length and correctly (lawful, adjudicated on the public record, the grant and two renewals on paper) and came to Karis afraid she'd been [P] "boring". Karis: you were accurate at speed; boring is the job; be boring a year.
6. That night: Cael opens Hesk's book at the ruled column with four names at its head (from M2) and fills it for the first time — three of the four have a line; his own has none. Log: I wrote that it should not cost them; it costs them first and me nothing yet. The order is the thing I got wrong.

## Ch17 — Under the Gallery (days 46–47)

1. Day 46 Fourth-day: sitting week, so the provision's bout is moved to the Fourth-day. Wing three has a narrow old gallery across its west end over the door (when the hall was the house's examination room; lately stacked with benches). The porters cleared it on the wing clerk's order because guests had signed the lodge book. Four visitors in it with boards: two from Rhagen (the house that writes everything down and sells it), one from the lake house, one from a northern house. Rooke at the east rail beside Ephram, arms folded, not there by schedule.
2. Cael's rotation line written last night (own: the first exchange to the Current, two bursts only, the spark once; buys a figure near the middle). The problem: strangers comparing him against a printed record. Look exactly like the house's record — not more, not less, not the same as last week either.
3. The bout (~1,800): a Current Path third-year, Iron Rank Four, long gliding steps that carry her farther than her legs should; two glides, the second bending, a strike off the bend. E1: he reads her weight, sees the bend coming, could burst inside it — gives her E1 as the line says; takes the strike on the forearm the honest way. E2: he uses the read to stand where the bend ends; one burst; touch. E3: she adapts — glides straight, no bend; he spends the spark on her lead wrist; no touch; glass runs. E4: she shortens to a single glide; he must not use a third burst? (line allows two); he takes ground on foot, lets her come to the wall, touch on her shoulder as she turns. Two to one. Throughout: the Rhagen pencils moving when he moves. He feels the band as a costume the strangers are measuring.
4. Figure: Gault adds the cards — twenty-two and two-thirds. Ephram writes it, no question mark this time; then, quietly: "They wrote the same number I did. That's the first time anybody's agreed with me about you." Rooke goes straight out. By noon the east hall's shutters are drawn, the gallery stair chained, the program drills under lamps. Lira at dusk: he closed the floor; didn't tell me why; told the clerk to keep logging. Her light gone. Cael's column gets a second line under her name.
5. Evening: Umber's transmission as a courtesy copy — the adjudication office's referred file sent entire to the proceedings (the record, the nine charts, the unscorable entry [canon: *Result: stoppage, Daeva, fourth exchange. Performance rating: unscorable under standard. Referred.*]) with a covering note (own text, three sentences: the file is sent entire; it is the whole of the office's finding; the office has nothing to add and asks nothing be added in its name). Karis maps it: once taken up the office had no choice; the third sentence is the only free act he had, and he used all of it; the clerk must read covering notes into the minute.
6. Day 47 Fifth-day, the second sitting: an exhibits sitting. The clerk reads the house's 173 items into the minute — title, date, provenance, receipt. Cael times it: short ones nine seconds, certified ones twenty; Prynn's index; Gault's minutes (the grant and two renewals) together just over a minute; the inspection finding twenty-two seconds; Karis's forty pages, nine. Fifty-one minutes for all of it. Nobody disputes a line; Jent doesn't rise once except to enter Umber's file (one exhibit, cited by number). By minute forty a third of the gallery has stopped listening. Havel at the junior chair keeps times, does not look. Log: the truth goes in unopposed and puts the room to sleep; they've built a room where our best work is a delay.
7. Counter, afternoon: Withrow signs the answer's release to three law faculties that asked; Bracken objects mildly (a respondent's filing is the respondent's). Withrow: a perfect filing nobody reads is a private loss; libraries outlast seats.
8. Log: the honest men — Umber, Vastin, Ilsev — each wrote the truth, and each truth is now an exhibit for the petition. The machine runs on true reports the way a mill runs on a river: it doesn't ask where the water wanted to go.

## Ch18 — Fifteen Words (days 47–49)

1. Day 47 supper. Bracken leaves his counter with nobody at it (first time Cael has seen it) and walks a sheet up to the residence faster than his own pace. Lays it before the counsel; stands; won't take Withrow's chair. The counsel reads it aloud: a statement entered at the seat in Daeva's own name and rank (own header: Daeva, of Auremont, Storm Path, Gold Rank Three, on her own account), then [P] *I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.* Silence. Lira sets her cup down. Brom reads it and turns it to face the room. Ephram: Auremont has a faculty for forty-page filings. Withrow: forty pages is an argument and arguments can be answered; this is a witness, from the top, and the code has no answer because nobody at her rank ever bothered. "Registrar. In your thirteen years?" [P-adj] "Never. Not at Gold." Four marks; a receipt.
2. Karis, annex, later: a provision older than the standardizing lets anyone Silver or higher enter a statement on evaluation standards; used a few dozen times, by Silvers; never by a Gold — Golds are what the statements are about. *Classify the instruments* has no form. Will the seat care? No. The record will.
3. Day 48 morning: Cael at the post room early. Seln is doing the sort (the post porter's shoulder). Seln hands him a pile to hold without a word; Cael watches how he sorts: three piles from the district courier's bag. Daeva's letter comes through Seln's hands — the hand from the *Overdue* form — Seln lays it on Cael's pile without looking at it. Cael reads it at the window: [P] the letter verbatim. Four readings. The one person the machine loves, telling him what the love is worth.
4. Log: [P-adj] *Keep yourself unclassified* — from her, not a joke; a strategy; filed. He files it behind the front board of Hesk's book.
5. Day 48 evening, Lira at the rail of the Crown yard: Zerin told her "Silver bracket. Next cycle." and walked off; she's been building toward it since. Daeva just did the same for him, from the top, on registry paper: here is where I'll be standing. Auremont builds them strange.
6. Day 49 Seventh-day morning: Seln at the lodge counter for his quarter hour (the code lets the wing read the lodge book on the seventh day); Cael with him, uninvited, allowed. Tea; Seln talks to the lodge-man about the lodge-man's quarrel with a carter over a scraped gatepost. The book: arrivals, and the road column the lodge has kept every bell since a year of thieves (own detail). Cael's own week moved four times (down the bluff with Brom at an odd hour; the river path before light; the ferry landing with Karis's cases; the sitting). The road column: two at the foot, changed on the bell, every bell, unmoved. Cael gets it himself on the walk back: a watch that never shifts when its man shifts isn't on the man. [P] "He is holding a road." Seln: "Mm."

## Ch19 — The Shape (day 49)

1. Supper: Seln gives the table the finding, to the teapot, plain as an inventory. The two at the foot are holding the road and nothing else; the watching has moved since the second sitting onto what the boy is attached to. Four items: (i) a records request at this house's counter three weeks ago, its fourth item asking for every institutional relationship with the enrollee (Bracken answered completely, twice saying so); (ii) a query at the district seat on Lira's certification history "for completeness of the respondent's file"; (iii) a query at the provincial registry over Velmere on the household's standing and registered relationships; (iv) a query at Ternhall on Karis's transfer file. Each lawful; two of them the sort a keen junior files unasked. The pattern is the finding.
2. Lira: her file has the Fenmark entry at the bottom, corrected over and never removed; a clerk read both halves in an afternoon. "I'd like it noted they've read it." Karis notes it. Brom's only question: [P] "Does the query show at the estate's end?" Seln: the provincial ledger is open to anyone entitled to the file; your grandmother is entitled. Brom nods once. Karis: the Ternhall one is twice. She writes four offices, dated, and rules one empty line under them.
3. Lira on the stair: why the blank? Karis: four is chance, his family would make it a document; they haven't reached them yet; whoever is building this is good.
4. The evening post, two hours later: the post porter puts a Denvash letter on top — he knows Hesk's upright address. Cael opens it walking on the covered walk (he opens only Hesk's walking; they've never been bad news). First paragraphs: Hesk's shop (own: a viol whose neck was set wrong by its maker and loved that way by its owner; Hesk spent days deciding to keep the fault; a price; the stove). Third paragraph: he stops on the walk, reads it again, goes to the residence at a pace nobody has seen from him in a year, and reads it aloud at the window: [P] *Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash…* Silence. He can't stop building the shop scene: front door, the bell, Hesk from the back with spectacles pushed up, answering exactly, offering nothing. Somebody wrote *grew up in it* on a form.
5. Lira: "They went to Denvash." Seln lays one finger on the blank sheet in front of him where a fifth line would go. Karis writes the fifth office in her blank line.
6. Seln names it, flat, his old trade: [P-adj] pre-restriction inventory. Before chapter fourteen's restriction executes, the registry maps everything the person's standing touches, so all of it shuts on one morning. Only run when the outcome is assumed. Karis: how long? "Six to ten weeks for an ordinary file. This one: [P] nine weeks. They are in the sixth." When finished: nothing — five lawful files in five offices; if the ruling goes his way, a clerk clears them in a few years and nobody did wrong; if not, assembly is an afternoon.
7. Karis: the four were about us; the fifth is a man who has never been near an academy. He's in it because the ninth category is *other relationships* — the shape has to close round everything Cael wouldn't want touched. Same machine, second use. "Say out loud that we noticed." Brom: "Noticed."
8. Lira declines the premise: Karis wins filings, the counsel wins sittings, Cael wins every point; their men are in Denvash. [P] "The question is what we do the day after." Nobody answers. Seln's silence is a different register.
9. Withrow's office that night: Cael with the letter; she reads it standing; a pause mid-page (he counts it). Trade licences and title, on an instrument-maker. She sits, and sits him down (neither usual). The second restriction of her two — she ran its inventory herself, the district short of hands. Force Path, Bronze Rank Four, a haulage practitioner in a river district, wife, two children, a house mortgaged; his trouble a twenty-year-old station error nobody living could correct; the re-evaluation returned nothing. Nine categories, counted off (employment; guild standing; credentials; property; money and debts; enrollments, his own and his dependents'; travel endorsements; office or contract; other relationships). Seven weeks; proud of it. The morning: nothing happened *at* him — nine letters from four offices by the post; contract suspended, run given to another, the house's note frozen, the daughter's enrollment under review, the transit endorsement lapsed so he couldn't leave to argue. He came to her counter on the fourth day with all nine in a bag and asked which to answer first. One problem with nine faces and no counter to answer at — a condition, not an act. He went east past the last station in the end; records stop there. The daughter came every week for a year (matches M1). Hesk: *grew up in it*, entered under the ninth category, true; a licence held by a relation of a restricted person may be reviewed; reviewing is only slow. How slow? As slow as anyone likes. [P] "Not a hand on the door. A hand on the clock." She at the window: still choosing; it costs more from tonight; priced and paying (own words).
10. Log, one pass: the first pole cracking — I win sittings, she wins filings, and their men are measuring my grandfather's shop; which part is the system working? The line between the system and the people is getting hard to find.

## Ch20 — Lot Seventeen (day 54)

1. First light, the coach down; the cases. Karis at the ferry: we'll win on the text; brace for winning feeling like winning. Lira: you said that last time. Karis: I'll say it every time; saying it keeps a finding from rotting into a hope. Brom's pocket; Seln with eyes shut; the counsel adding to her minute. Withrow ahead in her carriage.
2. The wool exchange: the four steps; the lot numbers cut in the boards; Cael's seat over *seventeen* again (he's sat on it every time). The covers are off the five evaluation chairs. Ilsev in the middle chair, files squared at their unchanging angle, sharpening a pencil into a paper fold so no shavings fall. Empanelment read: the evaluation seat constituted, the district's senior evaluator presiding (own wording). Havel at the junior chair, not looking. The hood; the hands.
3. The clerk reads Daeva's statement into the minute; the seal receives it; nobody rises; set aside, not contemplated. Cael hears the rhyme with Vastin's two sentences: credentials were never the coin; the ladder isn't what listens.
4. Jent, forty minutes, no papers in hand: concedes the enrollment's execution at once; the provision; the documentation (repeats his compliment for the minute and says not to soften it). Then four numbered points: (1) the integrity provisions protect the registry's confidence, their opening line read; (2) a practitioner no standard evaluation reads is one the registry cannot have evaluated confidence in — "by definition", defined once; (3) the demonstration provision describes, it doesn't evaluate — he begins "produces no record", stops, says that is too broad and false, asks the clerk to strike it, restates narrowly (a record of a different kind); (4) the discretion to compel re-evaluation is the registry's and needs no defect. Asks the seat to find the question cognizable. Cites Umber's file by page. Sits. Cael: courteous, honest, unhateable; a good instrument used well; the file to open isn't his.
5. The counsel, small and grey with her steel rule, rises and argues in four signposted movements: the text (three reconstructions, the directive's transitional schedule lists what it cancels and the clause isn't there); the precedent (Greyvane and the four rulings); the integrity provisions (she reads their preamble aloud — ninety seconds nobody in the room has heard; the ninety-one: eighty-four about things, seven about persons and all seven about conduct, none about what a person is). The seal's one question: incapable in law, or never so applied? "The second, and the second is enough" — own wording; Jent rises: he accepts a minute entry that it is novel; she repeats it to the gallery. Fourth: the demonstration record as evaluation — the grant and two renewals on paper, a season under a rating instrument, a tournament, the Compact's own inspection that declined to find; twenty-two officers who assessed him, named one by one into the minute, four minutes, silence. Karis's two cards: a struck page number; *the fifth point isn't needed* — not taken. Closing (own words).
6. Noon adjournment in the lanolin corridor: Withrow gives half a roll to a runner. Brom: so the men who listed what they cancelled left it off the list? Karis: that's the first movement. Why two hours? Because they might be right about the second thing. Havel passes with folders; Cael steps aside; nothing said.
7. The ruling, read by the hood from a page drafted in the adjournment: the execution sound; the provision valid and unrepealed; the record complete and undisturbed; the house compliant with its charter — the gallery begins to move — notwithstanding, the integrity question cognizable within the registry's discretion; sustained to full proceedings; re-evaluation to be heard on the merits; ruling before the year's close. Both at once. Cael reads the room in the four seconds after.
8. Ilsev asks leave; asks the seat to confirm the evaluation seat's scope: the conduct of any re-evaluation, not its outcome. "And not whether the protocol can evaluate this practitioner." Not within scope. She asks it entered in those words; it is; she enters it in her own file too. Cael reads it: she will certify the corridor's conduct and has no say over where it goes.
9. The steps: four couriers away inside three minutes. Jent pauses by the counsel: own honest compliment on one movement; "So did the seat." Hat; walks. The counsel: twenty-six years, the first opponent who tells her on the way out what she got right; costs him nothing; still true. Bracken: nothing left to concede. Counsel: there's always something when conceding is how you advance.
10. Coach home in the last light. [P] "We won every point that can be won on paper." / "And?" (Brom) / "And it's proceeding." The counsel's minute line upside down. The Archmarshal's absence; the seal's presence. Ferry crossing, one log line: [P] *Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.* Seln in the corner, eyes shut: [P] "Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

