     1	# Chapter 6 — Public Record
     2	
     3	The proceedings were public record, and the enrollee was the most-reviewed match result in tournament history, and the two facts met in the term's third month the way weather fronts meet, with the collision audible from every direction at once.
     4	
     5	Cael charted it because it was the largest apparatus of attention ever pointed at him and cataloguing was how he kept his footing in weather. The broadsides had the challenge within a week of its service — Withrow's one-line answer had guaranteed that; a chancellor who wrote her own document expected it read — and they built the story out of the parts they had, which were the Norhold interpretations with a registry seal added: the concealed-classification theory, now *confirmed* (the challenge proved the registry suspected it); the fraud theory, now *vindicated* (why else compel re-evaluation); the suppressed-Path theory, now the darkest and most popular (the Compact was covering its own error). A dozen versions, printed in four cities, every one of them still wrong, and every one of them now carrying, in its final paragraph, the phrase *evaluation-integrity question* as though the phrase meant something its authors understood.
     6	
     7	The economy of it was the part he could actually chart, so he charted the economy.
     8	
     9	Karis's assistant, sent down to Ostrand on an archive errand in the month's first week, came back with the whole market on four sheets and a receipt, because Karis had told her to buy one of everything and account for it, and because the girl had understood that *account for it* meant something more than the sum. Cael read her sheet at the annex table with real admiration. Six titles in Ostrand alone. The assay transcript, still on sale at six marks, two years old, in its fourth printing. A new pamphlet at three marks — *The Halcenvane Question, With The Instrument Entire* — which reproduced the challenge's first page accurately and the rest of it from a clerk's recollection, wrongly. The woodcut at a copper and a half, unchanged, still showing a boy who looked nothing like him in a stance no practitioner had ever adopted. And two competing broadside series at a half-copper the sheet, issued weekly, whose entire business was the proceedings and whose sales, the print-seller had told her without being asked, had tripled in a fortnight.
    10	
    11	"She asked him what sold before the challenge," Karis said, reading her own assistant's sheet. "He said the tournament. She asked what sold before the tournament. He said nothing; there was no series. He said the series was started *for* you, in the spring, by a man in the wharf street who bet his press on it and has now bought a second press." She set the sheet down. "There is a man in Ostrand whose children eat because you cannot be classified. I want that in your notebook and not in mine."
    12	
    13	Cael put it in the notebook. He put beside it the number the sheet made available for the first time, which was the one he had wanted for two years and had never been able to price: at a half-copper a sheet, two series, weekly, four cities — the continent was spending something on the order of a working man's yearly wage every week on the question of what he was.
    14	
    15	*Nobody is buying the answer,* he wrote. *There isn't one for sale. They're buying the asking. That's a market that doesn't close when the ruling comes down, and the men who own the presses know it, which is why the series that started in the spring is still running in the winter. Whatever the seat decides, it will be printed within four days, at a half-copper, beside a woodcut of a boy who isn't me.*
    16	
    17	The correspondents came up the bluff road twice that month and were declined at the lodge twice, courteously, on a standing instruction Withrow had issued in twelve words and Bracken had posted at the gate in the registry's own type: *the academy's answer to the challenge is its filing; the filing is public.* The second time, one of them waited four hours at the switchback in the cold on the theory that a boy of seventeen would eventually come down a road, and Rooke — who had gone down at dusk on his own business and had not been told to do anything at all — stopped, and looked at him, and said, "He trains at dawn and he doesn't come down. You'll get pneumonia and no copy," and the man thanked him and left, and that was the whole of the academy's press policy for the term.
    18	
    19	The post drowned Halcenvane. Bracken's counter took two sacks a day by the month's second week — academies writing to ask whether the demonstration provision was safe to invoke; guild houses writing to withdraw or affirm sponsorships they had never actually extended; three registry-law faculties writing for the response's text, which Withrow released whole, because a filing that perfect deserved readers; and the ordinary mail of a continent that had adopted a story, some of it kind, some of it not, all of it addressed to a boy who read every piece and answered none, per an information policy two years old.
    20	
    21	Bracken ran the counter the way he ran everything, which was to build a procedure on the second day rather than complain on the tenth. He put a second table in the records hall's outer room, split the intake four ways with a chalked card above each pile, and entered the day's count in the fire-watch's ledger every evening in his own hand, because the sacks were a fact about the institution and facts about the institution belonged in a ledger. Two hundred and eleven items on the heaviest day. Fourteen of them from chartered academies, which he answered himself, in four lines each, identically, and showed Cael one of the answers unprompted.
    22	
    23	"*The demonstration provision remains lawful and remains in force. The academy is not in a position to advise other institutions on their own enrollments.*" He blotted it. "Fourteen academies wrote to ask whether the clause is safe. Not one of them wrote to ask whether you are. I don't offer that as a comment on human nature. I offer it as a description of what a registrar's post looks like when a provision goes under challenge: institutions write about the provision." He tied the day's bundle. "The rest of the continent writes about the boy. Your friend is sorting that pile."
    24	
    25	Lira had adopted the sacks. It was the found family's oldest arithmetic — somebody carried what nobody could put down — and she sorted the post at the residence table each evening with the flat efficiency she gave bout sheets: the academies' inquiries to Bracken's tray, the sponsorship letters to Withrow's, the faculties' requests to Karis, and the rest into three piles she named aloud the first night and never renamed. "Kind. Unkind. Unhinged." The kind pile was the largest and the strangest — a barge-master's widow from the confluence who had watched him fight her husband's rival and wanted him to know the river was on his side; a Copper-tier washout from a hill academy who had read the transcript and wanted to explain, at four pages' length, that the corridor was real because he had walked one; a child's letter, in a schoolroom hand, asking whether it hurt to be unclassified. Lira read that one aloud, once, and put it in the kind pile, and nobody at the table said anything for a moment, and Brom refilled the cups.
    26	
    27	"Answer none of them," Karis said. "Keep all of them. When this is over, the kind pile is the only record of the year that wasn't written by an office."
    28	
    29	"You're a *case* now," Ephram observed, reading the day's sack at the residence table with the cohort captain's file forgotten under his elbow. "At Norhold you were a story. Stories end. Cases get *cited*."
    30	
    31	"I've been a case since Denvash."
    32	
    33	"You've been a *file* since Denvash. Files are private." Ephram held up a broadside from a city Cael had never seen. "This is a case. Three faculties are teaching you. Somebody at the coastal academy has written a thesis." He set it down. "Try to be a disappointing precedent."
    34	
    35	The cost landed on the others first, which Cael did not see for nine days and then could not stop seeing.
    36	
    37	It reached Lira at the rating table. The Silver program's clerk had begun logging visitors to the second floor's gallery — Rooke's instruction, unexplained — and the log ran to twenty-three names in a fortnight, of whom four were from academies with no business on the bluff and one had asked the clerk, in the ordinary way of a man making conversation, how often the unclassified enrollee came to watch. Rooke read the log, said nothing to the clerk, and moved the Silver drills to the closed floor for the rest of the month, which cost the program its high windows and its light. "He didn't tell me why," Lira said, at the rail, rolling the burst-shoulder while she said it — iced its two days, cleared for the frames on the Thirdsday, reporting clean under the closed floor's poorer light ever since. "He told the clerk to keep logging."
    38	
    39	It reached Brom in the Velmere post, which arrived that fortnight at three letters instead of two, and which he read at the table, folded, without comment, as he had read it since the recess.
    40	
