   127	He read it four times, which was the number he read the letters that mattered, and sat with it while the residence court went dark, because it was direct and without comfort and it was, he understood after the fourth reading, the truest letter anyone had ever written him about his own situation — and it had been written by the one person on the continent who had lived the situation's mirror. *Built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* She had said the sentence once before, at a ring's north arc, in the plural. She had said it now in the singular, on paper, to him, as the only credential she had left to offer: *I know what the machine's love is worth. I've had all of it.*
   128	
   129	*Keep yourself unclassified.*
   130	
   131	The log got the letter's whole architecture that night, and the reading:
   132	
   133	*She's the only person on the continent the system loves who knows exactly what its love is worth. "Keep yourself unclassified." From her, that's not a joke. That's a strategy. Filed.*
   134	
   135	Then he capped the pen and did the filing itself, which was the part the word had been standing in for: behind the volume's front cover, beside a page in Hesk's hand and a page in Vell's. Three pages, three people who had never met one another, each of whom had told him the same thing about the machine in a different grammar. He squared them against the spine, and closed the cover on the three of them, and set the volume down on the sill, squared to the edge.
