     1	# Chapter 7 — Assets
     2	
     3	The finding came to the residence table on a Fifth-day evening in the term's fourth month, and it came the way Seln's findings always came: flat, complete, in the administrative register, with a single sheet of the office's own paper in front of him on which he had drawn nothing, because a drawing could be found.
     4	
     5	What he did not give the table was how he had got it. He never did. So Cael took the finding apart afterward, the way he took a floor apart after a bout — from the thing shown, backward to the thing done — because a finding is a document and the method under it is a second document, and the second was the one he wanted.
     6	
     7	The method was the service side. Cael had watched the man walk it for a year and a half without once seeing it as a circuit: the delivery lane behind the refectory, where the bluff road's carts turned and the drivers talked while they waited; the stores counter, where every requisition in the academy passed under a clerk's initial and where the assessment office's own entitlement to read the stores ledger had never once been questioned, because nobody had ever thought to ask what an assessment office wanted with sacking and lamp oil; the post-room's morning sort; the gate lodge's arrivals book, which the office was permitted to inspect on the seventh day of each week and which Seln inspected on the seventh day of each week, standing at the lodge counter for a quarter of an hour with a cup of the lodge-man's tea, talking about the lodge-man's knee.
     8	
     9	None of it was surveillance. That, Cael understood a year and a half late, was its whole merit. A man who reads the arrivals book because the code says he may is not watching anything; he is doing his work, in front of the person whose work it is, with a cup in his hand.
    10	
    11	And on the seventh day of the third session's week the arrivals book had told him nothing at all, which was the finding — a negative, produced on schedule, by the only man who would have gone to a lodge counter looking for one.
    12	
    13	The next joint Cael could build because he had been its subject. Two men at the waystation, senior grade, the rotation running on the waystation's own schedule, and for four years the entire content of their work had been his own movement — where he trained, who he ate with, when he went down the bluff road and in what company. A following watch adjusts to its subject. It is the cheapest tell in the trade and nobody can suppress it, because a man told to follow a subject must go where the subject goes or file a lie. Seln had been keeping the adjustments since his second month at Halcenvane, unwritten, in the only archive that could not be requisitioned, and Cael could produce no evidence that the index existed except the accuracy of the sentence it had made.
    14	
    15	In the third session's week his own pattern had moved four times: down the bluff at an unscheduled hour with Brom, twice; the river path alone before dawn on a Fourth-day, which he had not run since the autumn; a Sixth-day at the ferry landing with Karis and two document cases, which was an itinerary a watcher would have paid for.
    16	
    17	The rotation had not moved once.
    18	
    19	*A watcher who does not adjust to his subject is not watching his subject,* Cael wrote in the notebook, and noted underneath that the sentence was Seln's and he had only found the shape it was cut to. *He is holding a road.*
    20	
    21	That was half of it. The other half Cael got out of the post-room over two mornings, without asking for it. The district courier's delivery went into three piles and Seln sorted them, because the clerk had a bad shoulder and Seln had never once been in a hurry. Two of the piles were the academy's. The third was the registrar's, and it was heavy, and it had been heavy on the courier's previous run, and on both runs the assessment office's pile had contained nothing from the district seat at all.
    22	
    23	The district seat had stopped writing to the office that watched him and had started writing to the office that held his papers.
    24	
    25	The last step the clerk supplied himself, unasked: Seln had put the piles down, said something civil about the shoulder, and carried the office's dispatch back across the fountain court at exactly the pace he carried it every morning.
    26	
    27	---
    28	
    29	The counter-scouting had never been formally asked for. It was the tournament autumn's pattern, continued into the winter by a man who audited surveillance the way other clerks audited floor schedules, because auditing was what his trade did with its hands when nobody was watching them; and the delegation had learned, across two years, to treat his findings the way instruments read the weather: without asking how the instrument worked.
    30	
    31	"The office notes a change in the observation's composition," he said, to the teapot. "The bluff-road rotation is unchanged — two men, senior grade, the waystation schedule holding. But the rotation is no longer the observation." He turned his cup a quarter-turn. "For four years the watchers watched the enrollee. Where he trained. Who he ate with. When he went down the bluff road and in what company. That work is done, and the men at the road are keeping it done, and that is all they are doing." He set the cup down. "Since the second session, the watching has moved. It is no longer on the enrollee. It is on everything *attached* to him."
    32	
    33	He gave the table the inventory, because inventories were his native document.
    34	
    35	A records request, three weeks old, at Halcenvane's own counter — routine in form, a district office confirming sponsorship files "for the proceedings' record" — which Bracken had answered per procedure and which had asked, in its fourth item, for the academy's full schedule of institutional relationships with the enrollee: sponsorship, floor time, evaluation-linked standing, travel endorsements. Bracken had answered it in twelve days, completely, with a covering letter that noted the completeness twice, because Bracken's whole doctrine was that a true record answered fully and let the office that read it own the consequences. Seln had read the office copy at the registrar's counter with the registrar standing beside him and had said nothing about the fourth item, and had thought about the fourth item for three weeks.
    36	
    37	A registry query, twelve days old, at the district seat, touching Lira's certification history — the Fenmark record, the Greyvane re-certification track, the Ostrand station's supersession — "for completeness of the respondent institution's file."
    38	
    39	A second query, nine days old, at the Velmere estate's provincial registry, touching Brom's family standing: the household's tier, the sister's guild placement, the estate's registered relationships.
    40	
