    73	---
    74	
    75	The counsel argued the response, and she argued it for two hours, and the term's discipline held all the way down, because the defense was *excellent*.
    76	
    77	She rose with papers and did not consult them. She was a big, unhurried woman of sixty with a voice built for stone rooms, and she had been Halcenvane's instructed counsel since the enrollment and had told Withrow at the retainer that she took institutional work because institutional work was the only place left where a lawyer could still lose slowly enough to learn something. She argued in four movements and she signposted every one of them, because a two-hour submission that a gallery cannot follow is a two-hour submission nobody remembers.
    78	
    79	*First, the text.* The assay clause's provenance: orphaned, not repealed. Three independent reconstructions — the academy's, the researcher's, and the district archivist's, commissioned by the counsel herself precisely because two agreeing reconstructions are a coincidence and three are a finding — arriving at one conclusion by three roads. She put the directive's own transitional schedule into the record and walked the seal through the schedule's structure item by item, and at the ninth item she stopped and said that the seat would notice, as she had, that the schedule enumerated the provisions it superseded and that the assay clause was not among them, and that the drafters of that schedule had been careful men, and that carefulness cut in only one direction.
    80	
    81	*Second, the precedent.* Greyvane's ruling, entered. Four prior challenges to demonstration-provision enrollments, all sustained on the text, entered with their citations. And the point she drew from the four: the demonstration provision's whole litigated history was a shelf of institutions losing to a clause they had failed to read, which meant the clause had been tested — repeatedly, adversarially, by well-resourced parties — and had held every time it had been tried.
    82	
    83	*Third, the integrity provisions themselves,* from Karis's forty pages. Their origin in the auditing of *instruments*: the counsel read the provisions' enacting preamble aloud, which took ninety seconds and which nobody in the room, by the look of the benches, had ever heard read. Their ninety-one recorded invocations, which the researcher had traced individually and tabulated by object — Arbiter frames, sixty-two; station calibration records, nineteen; evaluation protocols and their custody, three — and seven against an individual's conduct, and against what a practitioner *is*, exactly never. And the plain textual fact that a provision written to test whether an instrument could be trusted could not, without violence to its own language, be turned to ask whether a person could be.
    84	
    85	The seal put its only question of the session there. Whether the counsel contended that the provisions were incapable in law of application to a practitioner, or merely that they had not been so applied.
    86	
    87	"The second, and the second is enough," said the counsel, "because the seat is being asked to be the first in four hundred years, and a seat asked to be the first should be told so plainly, which the petition does not do."
    88	
    89	Jent, on his feet immediately, said that the petitioner had never contended the application was routine, and would accept a minute entry that it was novel.
    90	
    91	"The petitioner will accept a minute entry that it is novel," the counsel repeated, to the gallery for the first and only time in two hours, and sat back down into her fourth movement.
    92	
    93	*Fourth, the demonstration record as evaluation.* Four semester panels, sighted, sealed, renewed. A public season under a continental rating instrument. A tournament fielded under a charter clause, and an inspection by the Compact's own senior evaluator that had declined to find. More evaluation, she said, than any Iron-tier practitioner on the continent had ever received in the whole of a career, conducted by human beings with eyes, under seal, on the record, over three years, by twenty-two separate officers whose names she then read into the minute, one at a time, at reading pace, which took four minutes and which the gallery sat through in complete silence.
    94	
    95	Karis passed one card forward during the third movement and one during the fourth. Cael read both upside down without meaning to, because reading upside down was a thing he could not switch off: the first was a citation correction, a page number struck and re-entered. The second said only *don't take the fifth point, it's not needed*, and the counsel glanced at it, and did not take the fifth point.
    96	
    97	And she closed with the sentence Karis had written for her and she had made her own: "The petitioner asks the seat to prefer the silence of an instrument to the testimony of every human evaluator who has ever assessed this practitioner. The seat should decline. The record is the evaluation. It has been for three years. The only thing that has changed is who is asking."
    98	
    99	She sat. The gallery — forty benches of correspondents and the registry-literate — did the thing galleries did when they had heard craft, which was a low sound, sustained. Ilsev wrote for a long time. The seal received it.
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	The midday adjournment ran an hour and the respondent's party spent it in the exchange's old wool-hall corridor, which had windows and a bench and a smell of lanolin two centuries had not got out of the stone.
   104	
   105	Nobody said the submission had been good. Bracken said the citation correction had landed cleanly. Withrow ate half a roll and gave the rest to a correspondent's runner who had been standing in the corridor for two hours with nothing. Lira and Brom had come down for the session and had sat in the gallery's fourth bench and had not been able to hear the first movement properly, and Karis explained the transitional schedule to them against the corridor wall in six sentences, with her hands, and Brom said "so the men who wrote the list of what they were cancelling didn't put it on the list," and Karis said "that is the entire first movement, yes," and Brom said "then why does it take two hours," and Karis said, "Because they might be right about the second thing."
   106	
   107	Havel came through the corridor at the adjournment's end with an armful of exhibit folders, going the other way. He was three feet from Cael in a corridor eight feet wide. He looked at the folders. Cael stood aside to give him the room, and Havel said "thank you" to the folders, and went on, and it was the only word that passed between them at Ostrand that term.
   108	
   109	---
   110	
   111	The ruling came after the midday adjournment, and it came in one paragraph, read by the seal in a registrar's voice from a page the seal had plainly drafted during the adjournment, and the paragraph did both things at once, exactly as Karis had said it would, and the doing was the corridor's first door closing on schedule.
   112	
   113	*The seat, having heard the petition and the response, finds as follows. The enrollment's execution is sound. The provision under which it was executed is valid law, surviving the standardization directive unrepealed, and the seat so holds. The documentary record of the enrollment is complete and is not disturbed. The respondent institution has conducted itself throughout in full compliance with its charter.*
   114	
   115	Every point. Every documentary point that could be won, won, in the seal's own type, on the record, forever.
   116	
   117	The gallery had begun to move at *is not disturbed* — the correspondents' boards coming up, one man on the second bench already half-risen to get a courier out ahead of the others — and the seal read straight through the movement without a pause of any kind, in the same voice, at the same pace.
   118	
   119	*Notwithstanding the enrollment's validity, the seat finds the evaluation-integrity question raised by the petition to be cognizable under the code's integrity provisions, the applicability of those provisions to a practitioner being a matter within the registry's discretion which the seat exercises. The challenge is sustained to full proceedings. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol will be heard on the merits at the schedule's remaining sessions, and the seat will rule upon it before term's end.*
   120	
   121	Both things at once. You are lawful. The machine will proceed.
   122	
   123	The half-risen man sat back down. Cael charted the room in the four seconds after the paragraph ended, because four seconds after a ruling is when a room tells the truth: the counsel writing without expression; Bracken's hands flat on the table; Withrow's chin at the exact angle it had held for two hours; forty benches of registry-literate people working out, at forty different speeds, that they had just watched a respondent win and lose in one paragraph; and at the petitioner's table, Jent, who did not smile, did not gather his papers early, and did not look across the floor even once — a man receiving a result he had been sent to obtain, in the posture of a man receiving weather.
   124	
   125	Ilsev's beat came at the ruling's end, and the session rationed it carefully, and she spent it as she had spent every beat of her career, on the text's edges.
   126	
   127	She asked leave to address the seat. It was granted. She asked the seal, for the record, to confirm the evaluation seat's scope.
   128	
   129	The seal confirmed it: the seat's mandate was to assess the *procedure* of any re-evaluation ordered, its conformity to protocol, its conduct; not the outcome.
   130	
   131	"And not," said Ilsev, "the question of whether the protocol is capable of evaluating this practitioner."
   132	
   133	"That question is not within the evaluation seat's scope," said the seal.
   134	
   135	"I ask that the answer be entered in the minute in those words," said Ilsev, "so that the seat's scope is not later understood to have included it."
   136	
   137	It was entered. Ilsev entered the confirmation in her own file as well, in her own hand, and squared the file, and Cael, watching from the respondent's table, read the entering the way he had read her at Greyvane — a textualist marking the text's boundary so that the text could not later be said to have meant more than it said — and understood, with the instrument's whole cold clarity, that she had just told the room, in the only language she had, that her seat would certify the corridor's *conduct* and had no authority over where it led.
   138	
   139	Adjourned. The seal rose; the room rose; Withrow inclined her head at the exact depth.
   140	
   141	The market street outside had four hours of afternoon left in it and the correspondents got their couriers away inside three minutes, which Cael timed from the top step, because the speed of a ruling's departure was a measurement and he had nothing else to do with the interval while the cases were brought down.
   142	
   143	Four couriers, four directions, all four riding before the gallery had finished emptying. By the following week the paragraph would be in print in three districts, and it would be printed accurately, and it would be understood wrongly by almost everyone who read it, because a paragraph that says *lawful* and *proceeding* in the same breath is not a paragraph most people have a shape for. Half the continent would read it as a win. The other half would read it as the beginning of a fraud finding. Nobody outside the room would read it as what it was, which was a door being closed politely with the respondent still inside and thanked for his documentation.
   144	
   145	Jent came down the steps behind them with his clerk and the untouched tabbed folder, and paused at the bottom, and said to the counsel, without ceremony, "Your third movement was better than the authorities you had for it."
   146	
   147	"I know," said the counsel.
   148	
   149	"So did the seat," Jent said, and put his hat on, and went up the market street on foot.
   150	
   151	The counsel watched him go the length of two shopfronts. "Twenty-six years," she said, to nobody in particular, "and that is the first opponent I have ever had who tells me what I got right on the way out. He does it because it costs him nothing. It's still true." She took her case from Bracken. "Brace yourselves for the next one. He'll concede more."
   152	
   153	"There's nothing left to concede," said Bracken.
   154	
   155	"There is always something left to concede," said the counsel, "when conceding is how you advance."
   156	
   157	
   158	---
   159	
   160	The coach took the river road up in the last of the light with the same seven it had carried down — the five of them, the counsel, and the chancellor — the ruling's paper in the counsel's minute-book and the day's closing exchange waiting for whoever would spend it.
   161	
   162	Karis spent it, evenly, to the window.
   163	
   164	"We won every point that can be won on paper."
   165	
   166	Brom, after the length of time Brom took: "And?"
   167	
   168	"And it's proceeding."
   169	
   170	Nobody added anything for a while. The river went by. Lira looked at the drill-book on her knee and did not open it, and Cael charted her not opening it, and charted Brom's forearms on his knees, and charted the counsel writing in the privileged minute the one line he could read upside down — *ruling: sustained on validity; sustained to proceedings; both* — and charted the Archmarshal's absence from the day, which had been total, an evaluator not contemplated by the schedule, and the seal's presence, which had also been total, and had no face.
   171	
   172	He had the night's log by the ferry crossing, and it was one line, because one line was the honest count: *Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*
   173	
   174	Then Seln, from the corner, eyes closed, in the voice of a man who had audited the machine from inside for fifteen years and had never once, until this term, heard the audit read aloud by people who had not been trained to it:
   175	
   176	"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

