     1	# Chapter 14 — Standing Distribution
     2	
     3	The porter was late with the pigeons.
     4	
     5	Vastin noticed it from the desk at a quarter past the second bell, because the court below his window was the one thing in the Compact's house that had never once been late in nineteen years. The man came out of his lodge at last with the paper bag and an apology in his shoulders, as if the birds had lodged a complaint against him, and they came down off the ledges in a grey shower and walked about his boots while he scattered the crumbs in his half-circle, the same half-circle as always, until the morning was set right.
     6	
     7	The Archmarshal turned back to his trays.
     8	
     9	There were three of them on the desk, in the order he had kept since before he had a desk worth the name: *in*, *held*, *done*. The long cabinet of shallow drawers ran down the wall at his left hand. The seal-press stood at the desk's corner, screwed down crooked, a little out of square with everything else in the room. He had fixed it there himself on his first morning, nineteen years ago, in a hurry, and had never straightened it. Every officer who came into the room looked at it once. None had ever mentioned it. He had come to think of it as a small test of manners that nobody knew they were sitting.
    10	
    11	His left hand lay on the blotter. It had begun, some years back, to dislike mornings. Lately it had added evenings to its list, and it did not care for this one either. He opened it and closed it, slowly, twice, and then he took the first paper out of the *in* tray.
    12	
    13	It was a return from a river station on the condition of its frames. One housing had a split seal and would be resealed within the month. He initialled it. The second was a district clerk's request for leave to attend his brother's wedding, which had come up to the Archmarshal's desk only because the clerk's superior was afraid of granting anything. He granted it, and wrote a line in the margin to the superior that would make him less afraid next time.
    14	
    15	The third paper was a bundle tied in grey tape, and it had a printed slip pinned to its face.
    16	
    17	*For the evaluator's file, per standing distribution.*
    18	
    19	He read the slip twice. Then he set the bundle on the blotter without untying it, and looked at it.
    20	
    21	For three years, every paper that touched this matter had come to his desk with a question attached. *Advisory requested. Evaluation invited. The Archmarshal's view is sought.* He had answered each of them, and signed each answer with his name entire. This paper asked him nothing at all. It had been sent to him the way a weather return was sent to every office on a list, so that nobody could afterward say they had not been told.
    22	
    23	He untied the tape.
    24	
    25	Inside lay the instrument itself, in a fair copy: a challenge to enrollment basis, filed against a house on a bluff above a river by the registry of the Guilds Compact. Beneath it was the house's answer, or rather its index, eleven pages of exhibit numbers, because the whole answer weighed more than a courier would carry for a courtesy. Beneath that was the seat's notice. And at the bottom, last, was the minute of a first sitting at Ostrand.
    26	
    27	He read the date on the minute, and then he read it again.
    28	
    29	The proceedings had sat. They had sat a day ago, in a wool exchange, under a seal, and confirmed their schedule, and risen. The evaluation seat was noted as *to be called*. The minute did not say by whom.
    30	
    31	He moved the bundle to the far corner of the blotter, out of the way of his elbow, and left it there. He took the remaining papers out of the *in* tray and did each of them first, every dull one, at his ordinary pace, because a man who reads the interesting thing first has already decided what interests him, and after that his judgment on the dull things belongs to somebody else. It was a rule he had made at twenty and broken perhaps three times since. He was not going to break it for a grey tape.
    32	
    33	The tray was empty by the third bell. Only then did he draw the bundle back across the blotter and begin on the instrument, at the first leaf, at his own speed, while the pigeons settled on the ledges outside.
    34	
    35	It took him most of an hour.
    36	
    37	He knew within the first four leaves what kind of paper it was. By the tenth he knew where it was going, and by the last he could have sketched its whole floor from the doorway, as he could sketch a station's after one look in. It conceded what could not be won. It claimed nothing it would have to prove. It aimed the integrity sections, which he had used all his life on frames and stations and the men who kept them, at a person. It asked for a re-evaluation under the standard protocol, whose answer every officer in the building already knew. And under the re-evaluation, waiting, it had chapter fourteen.
    38	
    39	Chapter fourteen he knew without opening the code. Twice in forty years a finding of his had set it going.
    40	
    41	Both findings had been sound. A station keeper in the hills had let a cracked housing read every Ash a rank high for six years, and had covered the crack with false returns. A district officer on the coast had taken money to enter a rank that no frame had ever read. Vastin had written each finding and sent it down the stairs, and in some office below whose door he had never opened, the chapter had run its three steps to the end. He had not watched them run. Watching was not his office.
    42	
    43	Some months after the second file closed, a letter had come up the stairs from the district officer's daughter. The hand was careful, and had plainly been practised on other paper first. She asked whether there would ever be a day when her father's name was struck from the list.
    44	
    45	He had answered it himself. The answer was no, and he had written *no*, because anything gentler would only have been a second sentence for her to wait on.
    46	
    47	He looked out at the court for the length of time he allowed himself for such things. The porter had gone in. The pigeons were walking about on the stones with the busy look of birds who suspected more crumbs. He let the letter have the same span of attention he gave the birds. When a pigeon flew up onto the lodge roof, that span was spent, and he turned back to the desk.
    48	
    49	He went back to the seventh paragraph.
    50	
    51	He had known it would be there before he found it. It was quoted whole, in the petitioner's type, set off from the rest by a little space above and below, as a careful clerk sets off a passage he is proud to have found. *The clause says what it says. The enrollment is lawful on its face and adjudicated at public record. If the Compact wishes practitioners of this kind not to compete, it should write different rules — not misread the ones it has.*
    52	
    53	His three sentences. And above them, the word the petitioner had chosen for what it was doing with them. *Adopts.*
    54	
    55	He sat back. He had signed a great many documents in his life, and he had always known exactly which ones. This was the first time he had found his own hand at the foot of a page he had never seen. He had told them that the old rules did not say what they wished. He had told them, if they wished something else, to write it down honestly. And here it was, written down, honestly, and his own sentence set at the top of it like a warrant.
    56	
    57	He got up and went to the cabinet and took the boy's file out of his own drawer. He did not trust his memory with a subject, and had never trusted anybody else's. A memory was not an instrument. It only felt like one.
    58	
    59	---
    60	
    61	The file had grown thick since the bluff, and most of the thickness was other people's: a season's figures under a regional registrar's seal, a sheaf of tournament papers, and a bundle of broadsides that some junior had clipped and filed under *public record*, with a woodcut on the top one that looked nothing like anybody.
    62	
    63	His own portion was nine leaves. He laid them out on the blotter in order.
    64	
    65	The observation schedule. His floor notes from three days on the bluff, in his own small hand, the instruments set up and failing and set up again. The frame that had stuttered and gone dark, and the boy standing in it without moving. And the inky clerk's ruled sheet from the room twelve feet by fourteen, the whole interview on one page: the minutes counted down the margin, and down the middle eleven questions, numbered. Near the foot, going the other way, the one question the boy had put to him. *What is the Compact evaluating? Me, or the provision?*
    66	
    67	He read the eleven in order. It took four minutes. They were good questions. He had read them perhaps six times since that day, and every time they had been good questions, properly ordered, each one opening the next.
    68	
    69	The twelfth was on no paper anywhere.
    70	
    71	He knew it exactly. The boy kept records, and had said so, and had said what they were for: *so I can't lie to myself later.* The next question, the obvious one, was what the records said. Any competent officer would have asked it. He had looked at it for less than a second across that small room and left it where it lay. A private book was not in the record. An officer of his height who reaches past his mandate because the reach is open to him has stopped evaluating and begun to collect, and Vastin had once spent ten years helping to take apart a man who collected. He had promised himself, in a corridor with rain on the window at the end of it, that he would not become one.
    72	
    73	He had been right not to ask. He was still right. He turned the thought over once more, looking for a flaw in it, and found none.
    74	
    75	What he found instead was a small cold fact sitting beside the thought. A finding built on eleven honest questions could be answered in a paragraph. It had been answered in one, the seventh. A finding built on the twelfth could never have been written, and so could never have been used. His care had left the proceedings exactly one thing of his to take. They had taken it.
    76	
    77	The nine leaves went back into their folder, edges knocked true on the desk. Then he took a sheet of the house's paper and drew the inkstand toward him.
    78	
    79	He was not a man who mistook writing for doing. He did not think of what he was about to write as a protest. It was an evaluator's procedure, and he knew its every step.
    80	
    81	There were two provisions in the code under which an officer might bring a finding to a proceeding he had not been called to. He found both without looking them up. The newer one was dull and sound. The older one had come down from before the code was made standard. It was three lines long, in the plain voice of an age when an evaluator's word had been most of what a proceeding was for. *Where an evaluator of the senior grade has examined the subject with his own instruments, his finding may be laid before the proceedings; and they shall receive it.*
    82	
    83	*Shall receive.* The old drafters had been fond of that word. He was fond of it himself.
    84	
    85	He cited both. One leg was not enough for anything he meant to stand.
    86	
    87	He kept the petition to a page. It asked a single thing: leave to lay before the evaluation seat a finding signed and sealed as his own. As his ground he gave the one fact that no other officer of the Compact could give, which was that he alone of all of them had sat across a table from the enrollee with an instrument. He gave his grade, the dates of the three days on the bluff, and the two citations. Nothing else.
    88	
    89	Before he forecast anything, he liked to have every case in front of him.
    90	
    91	He had done this four times before, in forty years. The nearest to this one he took first: a district that had challenged a house's enrollment, a dozen years back, and his finding had gone in against the district's own petition. The seat had received it and weighed it, and the district had lost on it; some weeks later its seat sent him a short letter saying as much, with no grievance anywhere in it. Before that, a hill station's frame that read high: leave inside nine days, and the frame had come out of its housing on his evidence alone. Before that, a guild hall's matter, and earlier still a sponsorship quarrel he could barely now recall. Every time, the leave had come inside a fortnight. Every time, the finding had been read.
    92	
    93	Four times asked. Four times received. It was a thin sample, and it was every case that existed.
    94	
    95	He wrote his forecast on a slip, as he did with any outcome he could not command. Leave within the week, as a courtesy to the grade. A request for his file. The finding entered at the next sitting. He dated the slip and put it in *held*.
    96	
    97	His clerk took the petition down to the dispatch ledger a little before noon.
    98	
    99	He had been in the outer office for nine years. He was a narrow, tidy man who wrote the Archmarshal's instructions into the ledger exactly as they were given, and never asked what they meant, and spent the rest of each morning not asking. Vastin valued him for it more than he had ever said.
   100	
   101	The clerk wrote the date. He wrote the petition's number. Then the pen stopped above the routing column, and stayed there.
   102	
   103	Vastin had come to the door of the inner room for a file. He saw the pen stop.
   104	
   105	"Registry seat, Ostrand," he said. "Proceedings. Institutional."
   106	
   107	"Sir," said the clerk, and wrote it.
   108	
   109	He did not say anything else. He did not need to. In nine years every paper that had left that desk for a proceeding had gone *from* one, a finding returned to the office that had asked for it. The clerk's pen had never once been asked to send anything toward a proceeding that had not sent for it. His hand had paused at the edge of an unknown road, the way a good horse checks at a strange gate.
   110	
   111	Vastin went back into the inner room and sat down, and noted the pause, and gave it a line in his head where he kept such lines. *Nine years, and the outer office has learned the direction this desk faces. This morning I turned it round.*
