   117	He answered a station's question about a frame that read slow in damp weather. He signed a certification for a coastal district and sent back another with a line explaining why not. The pigeons were fed at the same hour each morning. The left hand ached in the evenings and he let it.
   118	
   119	On the third day a young evaluator came up the too-steep stair from the northern district, with a file under his arm and the look of a young man who had argued with himself all the way up.
   120	
   121	He was perhaps twenty-five, and good, and frightened of being good in front of the Archmarshal. Vastin gave him the chair and made him lay the file out himself, because a young officer learns more from putting his papers in order before a senior man than from anything the senior man says.
   122	
   123	It was a Kindling file: a station entry from four years ago, with the practitioner's Path and a first rank written in a station clerk's ordinary hand, and beside it the young man's own floor notes from this season, three sessions with the practitioner standing in front of a frame. The two did not quite meet. What the practitioner did on the floor ran ahead of what the station had written of her, by a margin the junior had measured four ways and could not make go away.
   124	
   125	"It isn't large, sir," he said. "It's within tolerance on every measure. I know that. I wrote that." He touched his own notes. "But I don't like it, and I don't know why I don't like it, and I thought that was the kind of thing I ought to bring up the stair rather than leave in a drawer."
   126	
   127	"You were right to bring it."
   128	
   129	They went through it together for an hour. Vastin made him walk the station's entry line by line, and then his own notes, and then the tolerances for a practitioner of that age and Path. Everything was where it should be. The station's figures were sound. The junior's figures were sound. A young practitioner had grown faster than a station had guessed she would, which young practitioners sometimes did, and the gap between a guess and a girl was no one's fault.
   130	
   131	"Within tolerance," Vastin said at the end. "Sound record, sound notes. The margin is growth. Write it so."
   132	
   133	"And the recommendation, sir?"
   134	
   135	"Continue observation."
   136	
   137	The junior wrote it, and blotted it, and thanked him with real relief. He went down the stair lighter than he had come up it.
   138	
   139	Vastin kept the copy.
   140	
   141	He had not meant to. The junior had left it on the blotter, as juniors did, and Vastin had picked it up to put it in *done* and had found himself reading the station's entry again instead. The Path. The rank. The clerk's hand. The date, which was an ordinary date, and the station's seal, which was an ordinary seal. He read the line once more than he needed to, and then once more than that.
   142	
   143	There was nothing in it. He had just spent an hour proving that there was nothing in it.
   144	
   145	He put the copy in *held*. A minute later he took it out again and looked at it, and then he put it back. He did not write anything about it anywhere, because he did not have anything to write. It was not yet a thing with edges. It was only a line he had read four times without being able to say why, and an evaluator who entered every unease he could not name would have a file full of himself.
   146	
   147	He turned the lamp down and went home.
   148	
   149	---
   150	
   151	On the fourth morning after the petition went down, he heard the clerk before he heard anything else.
   152	
   153	He had been at the window with his first cup, watching the porter fold his empty bag along its old creases, and from the stairwell behind him came a tread he did not at first recognize as anybody's. It was a single step, and then another single step, quick and even, all the way up the flight from the bottom. For nine years his clerk had come up that flight in one rhythm only: a quick double step to the turn, a slower single step after, and at the top a held breath while he judged whether he would be interrupting. This tread took no breath. It came straight on.
   154	
   155	Vastin set the cup down on the sill. By the time the door opened he had arranged his face, and he was sorry, briefly and privately, that it had needed arranging.
   156	
   157	"From the registry seat, sir." The clerk held it out in both hands, as if it were warm. "It came by seat courier, not the post."
   158	
   159	"Thank you. Leave the door."
   160	
   161	It was registry-seat stock, heavy and white, and the seal on it had been pressed into the paper rather than inked. That was a grade of reply in itself. A seat that inked its seals was writing to a colleague; a seat that pressed them was writing for the file. He weighed the grade before he let himself see a single word. Then he read the two sentences.
   162	
   163	*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*
   164	
   165	He stood at the window with it a long time. The porter had gone in. The court was empty. He read it again, and a third time, and on the third pass something shifted, the way the figure in a puzzle-picture shifts and will not shift back. Until then he had been hearing an answer. Now he was looking at workmanship. And he knew the writer's school, because it was his own.
   166	
   167	He went to the shelf behind the desk and took down his letter-book.
   168	
   169	It held fair copies of everything that had left this room in nineteen years, and he did not have to search long. He found one from his eleventh year here, a refusal he had sent to a field assessor who wished to add a page of his own to a review that had not asked for it. *The assessor's additional view is not contemplated by the review's terms.* One sentence. No provision cited. He laid the open book on the blotter and set the seat's sheet beside it.
   170	
   171	They might have come off the same bench.
   172	
   173	He had written a great many sentences of that family. A station asking to reopen its own finding. A junior officer going round his superior with a worry. A district that wished to be heard twice. Each had been answered in the same neat way, with nothing in the answer that the person outside could argue with, take hold of, or appeal. He had thought it a decent way to say no. It did not pretend. It did not insult a man by claiming to have weighed what it had not weighed. He had taught it to juniors as a piece of courtesy, and had been a little proud of how well they learned it.
   174	
   175	Here it was, made to his own pattern and well made, and turned round to face him.
   176	
   177	He looked for the provision. There was none. His petition had stood on two, the old one and the new, and the answer stood on nothing. That was the whole of its meaning. The seat had not ruled against him, because a ruling could be appealed. It had simply declined to hold a ruling at all, and there is no appeal from an empty room.
   178	
   179	*Fully constituted.* He tried the words for weight. A seat he had not been asked to sit on had been full before he asked. Very likely it had been full before the first sitting, before the wool exchange's floor was waxed for it. Whoever had drawn the schedule had known what the evaluation would find, and had known that the one officer who had put an instrument to the subject might find something else, and had drawn the schedule with no room in it for something else.
   180	
   181	And it had cost its writer nothing. He was too honest an instrument not to read that too. Two lines on good paper, nothing in them to answer, nobody behind them to be answered. It would have taken less time to write than to sand. It was exactly as good as his own had always been.
   182	
   183	He closed the letter-book and put it back on the shelf. He did not put the denial in *held*. He opened the boy's file, laid the sheet on top of his own nine leaves, and shut the cover over both.
