   186	
   187	The clerk came up at the sixth bell for the day's wallet and found him writing on plain paper, which was not a thing the outer office often saw. The Archmarshal wrote in the mornings, on forms.
   188	
   189	"Nothing of mine tonight," Vastin said, before he could ask.
   190	
   191	The clerk took the wallet. He went as far as the door, and then he came back with a candle he had already lit in the outer office, in a tin dish, and set it on the corner of the desk beside the crooked press. The house put out the lamps on that floor at the half-bell after six. Nobody had ever needed to tell him that.
