   153	He had been at the window with his first cup, watching the porter fold his empty bag along its old creases, and from the stairwell behind him came a tread he did not at first recognize as anybody's. It was a single step, and then another single step, quick and even, all the way up the flight from the bottom. For nine years his clerk had come up that flight in one rhythm only: a quick double step to the turn, a slower single step after, and at the top a held breath while he judged whether he would be interrupting. This tread took no breath. It came straight on.
