    68	
    69	She came in the same way. Two glides and the bend.
    70	
    71	This time he used the read and not the burst. As her weight went into the outside of her front foot, he walked, at an ordinary walking pace, to the place where the bend would end. It was three strides. Nobody could have called it anything but timing. When she came off the curve he was already standing there, a little to the side of where she meant to arrive, and she saw him and checked in the middle of her glide, which a Current hates to do, because the whole of their Path is in not checking.
    72	
    73	She checked well. She cut the glide short and turned her strike into a sweep, low, at his front knee.
    74	
    75	He spent the first burst.
    76	
    77	Low and lateral, off the left foot, to her outside; the boards came up under him eight feet on and the landing beat held him there, behind her sweep, at her back. His right hand was on her shoulder blade before the sweep finished.
