   150	
   151	"And the inventory."
   152	
   153	"Nine categories." She counted them off on the desk with two fingers, not looking at her hand. "Registered employment. Guild membership and standing. Credentials and endorsements. Property held or part-held. Financial instruments and debts. Institutional enrollments, his own and his dependents'. Travel and transit endorsements. Public office or contracted service. And *other relationships.*" She stopped. "It took me seven weeks. I was proud of it, in the way you are proud of a thorough piece of work, and I have thought about that pride for twenty-five years."
   154	
   155	"What happened on the morning?"
   156	
   157	"Nothing happened. That's the answer. Nothing happened *at* him." Her voice did not rise; it flattened, which he had learned was the same thing. "I did not go to his house. Nobody went to his house. Nine instruments went out from four offices on one morning, and by the second bell his guild had suspended his contract because a guild may not contract an unclassified practitioner, and the haulage yard had put another man on his run, and his bank had frozen the house's instrument because the instrument named his standing as security, and his daughter's academy had written to say her enrollment was under review because a dependent's enrollment carries the parent's standing, and his transit endorsement had lapsed, which meant he could not lawfully leave the district to argue about any of it." She looked at the charter above the file cases. "He found out in the order the post arrived. He came to my counter on the fourth day with all nine letters in a satchel and asked me which one to answer first. I told him the truth, which was that they were not nine problems. They were one problem with nine faces, and the one problem had no counter to be answered at, because the restriction is not an act. It is a *condition.* There is nobody to argue with. That is the design."
