   185	"What did you tell him? At the counter."
   186	
   187	"The truth. That he hadn't got nine problems. It was one trouble that had come to his door nine times in nine different coats, and there was no counter anywhere he could stand at to face it, because a restriction isn't a thing anybody does to you. It's a condition you are left standing in." She closed the code. "Nobody did anything to him. Nobody had to. That's how it was built."
   188	
   189	Cael found that he had stopped breathing a little while ago, and started again.
   190	
   191	"What became of him?"
   192	
   193	"In the end he crossed into the east, beyond the last station. Men with families usually go, sooner or later. This side of the line has no room in it for a man with children and no classification. It took him eighteen months, which is what it costs to get round a lapsed transit endorsement and stay inside the law. After he crossed, I don't know. A man past that line stops making records, and records were the only eyes I ever had." She was quiet for a moment. "His girl stayed behind, with her mother's people. You know the rest of that."
   194	
   195	"She came to your counter."
   196	
   197	"Every week for a year."
   198	
   199	Withrow closed both hands round the little brown book.
   200	
   201	"Now," she said. "Your grandfather. *Grew up in it* is what he told them, so it's what they wrote, and it's true. It goes under the ninth heading, a true line among true lines, and it waits there until the morning somebody wants the ninth heading. Nobody will say a word against him. They won't need to. Any licence held by a man the registry has entered as tied to a restricted person can be called in for review." She did not raise her voice. "Nobody refuses it. They review it."
   202	
   203	"For how long?"
   204	
   205	"For as long as anyone likes," said Withrow. "That's the whole design. Not a hand on the door. A hand on the clock."
   206	
   207	She got up, then, and went back to the window, and stood with her hands behind her looking out at the dark where the bluff fell away toward the river. When she spoke again she said it to the glass.
   208	
   209	"I told you at the start of this that I'd chosen, and that I was choosing. Both are still true." He saw her reflection in the black window, quite steady. "Tonight it's dearer than it was this morning. I've worked out by how much. I'm going to pay it."
   210	
   211	---
   212	
   213	He wrote the fourth reading into Hesk's book at his own window, past midnight, with the letter flat on the sill beside the column.
   214	
   215	*I win the sittings. Karis wins the filings. The counsel wins every point there is, and the seat enters that she won.*
   216	
   217	*And two polite men have been to Denvash to ask an old man whether my name is in a tin box under his bench.*
   218	
   219	*Where in that is the system doing what it's for? For three years I've stood on one line: on one side the text, which holds; on the other the people, who fail, and can be read, and can be beaten. I've never once doubted where that line was. Tonight Withrow told me about a flour sack and nine letters, and I went looking for the line, and couldn't find it. A hand on a clock isn't a person. It isn't text either. I don't know what it is. I've never before needed to know.*

