   301	"You should have taken your fifth point," he said. "It was a good one."
   302	
   303	"It was. I didn't need it."
   304	
   305	"No." Jent put on his hat. "That's why I'd have liked to hear it." He nodded to her, once, and went off up the market street on foot, alone, like any man walking home to his dinner.
   306	
   307	The counsel looked after him until he turned the corner by the corn chandler's.
   308	
   309	"In twenty-six years," she said, "he's the only one who's ever stopped on the way down to mark my paper. It costs him nothing, so he's generous with it. It's also accurate, which is what I can't forgive." She took her case back from Cael. "He'll give more away next time."
   310	
   311	"Has he got anything left?" said Lira.
   312	
   313	"Watch him find something," said the counsel. "Giving things away is how that man walks forward."
   314	
   315	The coach went home along the river road with the light going off the water, behind Withrow's carriage, with the six of them inside: the five and the counsel.
   316	
   317	At the first milestone past the town Brom took the slip out of his right inside pocket. He unfolded it, read it, and held it up so that they could all see.
   318	
   319	One word, in Karis's hand.
   320	
   321	*Both.*
   322	
   323	Karis did not look at it. She was looking out of the window at the river.
   324	
   325	"We won every point that can be won on paper," she said.
   326	
   327	Brom folded the slip along its creases, taking the time he always took over anything he meant to keep. "And?"
   328	
   329	"And it's proceeding."
   330	
   331	Nobody said anything after that for a long way. The river slid by. Lira kept one hand on the closed drill-book and never lifted its cover, and Cael watched her keep it shut. He watched Brom put the slip back in the right-hand pocket and leave his hand there over it. He watched the counsel write one line into her minute, which he read upside down: *Valid: held. To proceed: held. Both.*. He counted the Archmarshal's part in the day, which was none at all, a name read aloud among twenty-two by somebody else; and the seal's part, which had been the whole of it, and had no face.
   332	
   333	At the ferry, while the coach waited its turn, he set the night's entry in his head. It came out at a single line, and he did not try to make it longer.
   334	
   335	*Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*
   336	
   337	Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. He spoke from his corner of the bench in the dry, level voice of the office, the voice of a man who had kept its accounts from the inside for fifteen years, and who had never until this year heard anybody outside that trade say the sum out loud.
   338	
   339	"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

